From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 30 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] S: register the USB classes before the host controller

`Run AddUSBClasses` is asynchronous, and this Startup-Sequence adds the host
controller by hand ninety lines later (patch 0014). Those two race, and the
loser is a machine with no mouse.

psdAddClass() does not rescan. A class registered after the last
psdClassScan() binds to nothing that is already there, and the only scans are
the one at the end of AddUSBClasses, the one in AddUSBHardware, and the one
hub.class runs per newly configured device. So when AddUSBHardware gets there
first, the devices behind the root hub are enumerated and offered to whatever
subset of the classes had managed to load -- and hid.class, 191 KB of the
megabyte AddUSBClasses has to read off the card, is not usually in it.

Upstream can afford `Run` because upstream does not add hardware here at all:
a USB dongle enumerates long after the stack is up, by which time the classes
have loaded whenever they liked. This port has the controller on the board and
names it explicitly, so the ordering has to be stated rather than assumed.

It costs about a second of boot, and it is the difference between a pointer
and no pointer.
---
 workbench/s/Startup-Sequence | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/workbench/s/Startup-Sequence b/workbench/s/Startup-Sequence
--- a/workbench/s/Startup-Sequence
+++ b/workbench/s/Startup-Sequence
@@ -35,9 +35,9 @@
 EndIf
 
 If EXISTS "SYS:Classes/USB"
     Assign "USBCLASSES:" "SYS:Classes/USB"
-    Run <NIL: >NIL: QUIET AddUSBClasses
+    AddUSBClasses >NIL: QUIET
 EndIf
 
 If EXISTS "SYS:Classes/Bluetooth"
     Assign "BTCLASSES:" "SYS:Classes/Bluetooth"
